WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:03.200
So first off, the server-client model.

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.200
We've talked about this in previous sessions,

3
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:07.200
if you've watched any of the previous ones,

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.439
but Unreal operates on an authoritative server model.

5
00:00:11.439 --> 00:00:17.039
So what that means is that really what the server says goes,

6
00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:21.000
the server maintains the actual true state of things.

7
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.199
Obviously, there's a discrepancy here when you're not doing multiplayer games.

8
00:00:26.199 --> 00:00:29.199
That's referred, let's say, kind of like,

9
00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:31.199
configuration that's referred to as standalone.

10
00:00:31.199 --> 00:00:34.200
That essentially means you are playing the game locally,

11
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.200
meaning the client and server are essentially the same thing.

12
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.200
So in the situation of single or local multiplayer,

13
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:46.200
there is a single, the client machine is essentially the server's machine.

14
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.200
There's really no server.

15
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.200
But it's worth mentioning that very, very early on,

16
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.200
we talked about the idea that we had,

17
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.200
I think this is in the first training session,

18
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.200
we have these different pragmas that you could use to define

19
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.200
whether or not the code you're working in is server code or client code.

20
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.200
And that is true when you're in standalone or when you're in editor,

21
00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.200
that is true for both, both of those things are true.

22
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.200
It is both client and server.

23
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.200
So hypothetically, in standalone,

24
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.200
when you're playing just a single-player game or a local multiplayer,

25
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.200
you are kind of a client and a server.

26
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:24.390
So in that situation, the client is authoritative.

27
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:28.390
We'll talk about what authority specifically means shortly.

28
00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:31.390
But yeah, it has full control over everything.

29
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.390
All the data is local.

30
00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.390
We don't have to worry about data going to any other computers,

31
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.390
to any other clients or the server.

32
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.390
And we don't have to worry about data importantly coming from anywhere.

33
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:44.390
Then we have the kind of other situation, which is a network setup.

34
00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:49.390
And what that means is the server is always fully authoritative

35
00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:54.390
and players that connect to the server are kind of referred to as clients.

36
00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:58.390
And as we said, it's the server that holds the true state of the game.

37
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:05.390
In that situation, the kind of all relevant gameplay logic

38
00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:10.389
is and definitely should be local to the server.

39
00:02:10.389 --> 00:02:15.389
But there are aspects of data that would be kind of remote

40
00:02:15.389 --> 00:02:17.389
that either the server just doesn't care about

41
00:02:17.389 --> 00:02:20.389
or technically the client would have authority over.

42
00:02:20.389 --> 00:02:22.389
Again, we'll talk about those in a little bit.

43
00:02:22.389 --> 00:02:25.389
And then there's also another configuration to kind of bear in mind

44
00:02:25.389 --> 00:02:30.389
where one of the players can be simultaneously the client and the server.

45
00:02:30.389 --> 00:02:33.319
We'll talk about that a little bit more in a bit.

46
00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.319
So the types of servers.

47
00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:39.319
We've got dedicated, which is the kind of standard setup

48
00:02:39.319 --> 00:02:43.319
for a lot of the kind of competitive games you'll be playing online.

49
00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:48.319
So in this context, the server runs an entirely distinct machine.

50
00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:51.319
The server would run in a kind of headless configuration

51
00:02:51.319 --> 00:02:53.319
some people might refer to it as.

52
00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:56.319
This essentially means that the server doesn't need to be doing any of the things

53
00:02:56.319 --> 00:02:59.319
you as a player would typically care about in a game.

54
00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:01.319
It doesn't need to be listening for input.

55
00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:02.319
It doesn't need to render visuals.

56
00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:06.319
There are no local clients on this machine.

57
00:03:06.319 --> 00:03:10.319
So all the server is really there to do is process input changes

58
00:03:10.319 --> 00:03:14.319
like messages that might be coming from clients

59
00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.319
kind of ticking the game over

60
00:03:16.319 --> 00:03:19.319
and just maintaining that authoritative state of the game

61
00:03:19.319 --> 00:03:24.539
and then dispatching that information to each of the connected clients.

62
00:03:24.539 --> 00:03:26.539
It receives information from clients to verify,

63
00:03:26.539 --> 00:03:29.539
so that might be things like inputs and then clients informing them

64
00:03:29.539 --> 00:03:32.539
where the client thinks that their location is.

65
00:03:32.539 --> 00:03:35.060
And then alternative to that, we have the listen server.

66
00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.060
This is what I was just referring to a minute ago.

67
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:42.060
This is essentially where a single client will also act as server.

68
00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.060
So this is not really a suitable setup for anything

69
00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.310
that is meant to be certainly competitive games

70
00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:56.310
or anything with a lot of players playing locally,

71
00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:59.310
with a lot of players playing in a single session.

72
00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.310
It's not because of the kind of additional costs

73
00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:04.310
of rendering a screen, listening for input,

74
00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.310
hosting on additional kind of client configuration.

75
00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:10.310
There's only a kind of, there's a more limited subset of situations

76
00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:13.310
where a listen server is really acceptable.

77
00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:16.310
Most of the time that's going to be probably more cooperative games

78
00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:22.310
because it's probably relatively obvious that if you are a listen server,

79
00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:24.310
your ping is going to be close to zero.

80
00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.310
So you're going to have a massive advantage

81
00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.310
over other players connected to your game.

82
00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.310
So if you're in a competitive environment,

83
00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:33.310
that's probably not going to be suitable.

84
00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.310
And also when you're, even if you are doing a,

85
00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.310
some kind of cooperative game at scale,

86
00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.310
purely the extra cost put on your CPU

87
00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:45.310
of having to do all those kind of local client calculations,

88
00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.310
it's just going to mean that it's not necessarily

89
00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:48.310
such a suitable configuration.

90
00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:52.310
So dedicated server is going to be suitable for all situations.

91
00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.310
Listen server is admittedly easier to set up,

92
00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:00.310
but is really only going to be suitable for probably non-competitive,

93
00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:03.310
probably small scope, like no more than, you know,

94
00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:06.310
four to eight players maybe, kind of set up.

95
00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.470
The other thing about dedicated server,

96
00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.470
you might be familiar with this term is that, you know,

97
00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:15.470
some games would choose to give out a copy of their game

98
00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.470
where you can turn your own computers into dedicated servers.

99
00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.470
So it depends, that's kind of a design decision on you as a developer

100
00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.470
of whether or not you want to expose the functionality

101
00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.470
to host, for players to host their own dedicated servers

102
00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:31.470
or whether you want, you know, the acquisition of dedicated servers

103
00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.470
to be something that you purely do as a kind of proprietary thing

104
00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.470
where people would have to either purchase servers

105
00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:39.470
or you as a developer take on the responsibility

106
00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.470
of hosting all dedicated servers for your game.

107
00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.910
So just talking about like how someone actually, you know,

108
00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:49.910
when a client is trying to join, connect to a server,

109
00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:53.910
let's just talk a little bit about how that process actually goes.

110
00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:59.910
There's actually a very good post in Unreal documentation,

111
00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.910
which I can kind of come back to, that kind of goes through

112
00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.910
a little bit in kind of more detail, but in the kind of like,

113
00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:09.910
in the broad scope of things, yeah, we just kind of go,

114
00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:11.910
worth going over this kind of high level flow.

115
00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.910
So the first step is we have our client make a request

116
00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:16.910
for a connection to our server.

117
00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:19.910
At that point, the server can reject,

118
00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.910
accept or reject that connection request.

119
00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.910
There's some kind of like handshaking that goes on.

120
00:06:24.910 --> 00:06:26.910
And then once the connection is accepted,

121
00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.910
the server is going to send information down

122
00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.910
to the client for them to proceed.

123
00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:33.910
At that point, the server is going to kind of

124
00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:35.910
tell the client what map to load

125
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.910
and then proceed to wait for the client to load the desired map.

126
00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.910
And once the server receives that callback,

127
00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.910
the pre-login function is called in the game mode.

128
00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.910
We talked about this in I think our second session

129
00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:48.910
about the kind of game flow.

130
00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:51.910
And it's critical step, this kind of pre-login function,

131
00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.910
because it's at that point that you can inject gameplay code

132
00:06:55.910 --> 00:06:58.910
that can kind of choose to accept or reject that connection.

133
00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:01.910
So whilst we said the server decides to accept or reject

134
00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.910
the connection very early in the flow,

135
00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:05.910
that is not something that you as a developer

136
00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:07.910
would typically have access to

137
00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.910
unless you went changing engine code.

138
00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:11.910
The kind of point at which you as a developer

139
00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:14.910
would typically inject that the kind of capability

140
00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.910
to reject or accept a connection

141
00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:19.910
is going to be in the game mode base pre-login function,

142
00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:21.910
which you can obviously override

143
00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:23.910
in your game mode classes.

144
00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:27.680
If that is then accepted by your pre-login,

145
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:30.680
we then go on to call login game mode.

146
00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.680
And it's a login that we would call,

147
00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.680
we would create the player controller on the server.

148
00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.680
And it's, you know, as with all of this flow,

149
00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:42.680
we'll talk a little bit more about this,

150
00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:45.680
but whenever you create actors on the server,

151
00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.680
those actors are then kind of inherently replicated to the client.

152
00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:52.680
So that login function will create the player controller on the server

153
00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.680
and that then gets replicated to the client.

154
00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:00.680
It's also really important to note that the player controller

155
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.680
will get its begin play called.

156
00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.680
So it's just understanding, like it's important to understand

157
00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.680
where that's going to occur in that flow.

158
00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.680
It would be at this point.

159
00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.680
And then lastly, the server is going to call post login

160
00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:13.680
on the game mode.

161
00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.680
And that's basically the point at which it's now safe

162
00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.680
to call RPC functions on the player controller.

163
00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.680
You can expect those functions.

164
00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.680
We've not talked really about what RPCs are yet,

165
00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:29.680
but it's at the point of post login getting called

166
00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.680
that we can be certain that that connection is set up,

167
00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.679
that the client has that player controller,

168
00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:36.679
and therefore RPCs are going to kind of flow through to that client.

169
00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:42.679
As I said, there's a kind of more detailed post.

170
00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:44.679
I believe it's actually by a member of the community,

171
00:08:44.679 --> 00:08:46.679
not an Epic dev themselves,

172
00:08:46.679 --> 00:08:50.679
but it just goes into this flow in more detail

173
00:08:50.679 --> 00:08:52.679
and more granular function by function call.

174
00:08:52.679 --> 00:08:54.679
You'll actually see this in the presenter notes.

175
00:08:54.679 --> 00:08:59.679
So if you're more interested in more specific detail about this,

176
00:08:59.679 --> 00:09:06.679
there's a good post about this on the Epic community forums.

177
00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:09.679
And that's actually part of the presenter notes in this presentation.

178
00:09:09.679 --> 00:09:11.679
So if you're interested in following up on that,

179
00:09:11.679 --> 00:09:13.679
just follow that link in the presenter notes.
